"There are five surahs in the Quran that begin with praise (hamd): al-Fatihah, al-An'am, al-Kahf, Saba', and Fatir. Allah's blessings are of two kinds: origination (ijad) and continuation (ibqa). In each surah, reference is made to these two blessings. In Surah Saba', by recalling the blessing of the Hereafter and the recreation of life, it is emphasized that all praise belongs to Allah. He is the owner of whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. Both the blessings of this world and the blessings of the Hereafter are of His bestowal.\n\nAllah knows whatever enters into the earth and whatever comes out of it, and whatever descends from heaven and whatever ascends into it. He is the All-Wise (al-Hakim) and the All-Aware (al-Khabir). Those who deny the Resurrection do not deem it possible because they do not know the unseen (ghayb). Yet Allah's knowledge encompasses all things; everything is clear in the Manifest Book (Kitab al-Mubin). This knowledge and power necessitate the coming of the Day of Resurrection. That Hour will surely come in order to reward those who believe and do righteous deeds.\n\nThe disbelievers mock prophethood. Yet those who do not believe in the Hereafter are in pangs of conscience even in this world. Allah warns them: Do they not look at what is before them and what is behind them of the heaven and the earth? If We willed, We could cause the earth to swallow them up or cause pieces from the sky to fall upon them. Indeed, in this is a lesson for every servant who turns to Allah.\n\nThe virtue bestowed upon David (Dawud): The mountains and the birds glorified Allah along with him, and iron was made pliable in his hands. He used to make coats of armor. The melodious voice of David and the miracle of the Psalms (Zabur) are recounted. The spiritual value of a beautiful voice and melody is not merely physical. The essential point is to convey the meaning. This art is intended for beneficial endeavors, just as crafting protective armor for battle.\n\nTo Solomon (Sulayman) were given the wind and a spring of molten copper. The jinn also worked under his command. They made for him sanctuaries, statues, basins like reservoirs, and cooking pots fixed into the ground. Solomon's death was only realized through a wood-boring creature that ate away his staff. Thus, it became evident that the jinn do not know the unseen.\n\nThe cautionary tale of the people of Saba': Two gardens—while gratitude was demanded of them, they turned away. Thereupon, the flood of 'Arim was sent upon them, and their gardens were transformed into ruined gardens bearing bitter fruit, tamarisks, and a few lote trees (sidir). This was the recompense for their ingratitude. Thus does Allah penalize the persistently ungrateful.\n\nThe people of Saba' possessed towns close to one another between blessed lands. They used to travel in safety. However, they asked for the distance between their journeys to be lengthened and wronged themselves. Allah turned them into legendary tales and scattered them completely. Iblis's assumption about them proved true; most of them followed him. Yet Iblis had no real authority over them; the purpose was to distinguish the one who believes in the Hereafter from the one who is in doubt concerning it. And Allah is watchful over all things.\n\nThe people of Saba' lived in the city of Ma'rib in Yemen. There were for them two lush gardens that served as a sign. These gardens were vineyards and orchards flanking them on the right and on the left. It was said to them, 'Eat from the provision of your Lord and be grateful to Him.' Their land was fair and their Lord was Oft-Forgiving. But they turned away and did not show gratitude.\n\nThereupon, Allah unleashed upon them the flood of 'Arim.' Arim was a great dam built by Bilqis. The gap between two mountains had been closed with stones and pitch, and waters were collected. According to various narrations, the dam may have been built by Saba', Himyar, Luqman, or Bilqis. All of them may have carried out construction and repair in succession. When the dam collapsed, their gardens turned into bitter tamarisk, tarfa, and thorny trees.\n\nWe replaced their two gardens with trees bearing bitter fruit. Because of their ingratitude, their gardens were ruined. Furthermore, We had placed between them and the towns We had blessed visible towns situated side by side. Travel was safe and easy. But they did not want this blessing, saying, 'Our Lord, make the distance between our journeys longer.' So We scattered them utterly; the tribes were dispersed.\n\nIblis proved his assumption about them to be true. Except for the sincere believers, most of them followed Iblis. This was not due to any power of Satan, but rather due to their lack of faith in the Hereafter. Allah's command is supreme. Your Lord is watchful over all things.\n\nThose who invoke idols besides Allah should know that they have no power over anything. Intercession is only for those who are permitted. On the Day of Resurrection, when terror is removed from their hearts, the intercessors say, 'What did your Lord say?' And they reply, 'He spoke the truth.' Allah is exalted above all things.\n\nThe disbelievers said that they did not believe in the Quran nor in the scriptures before it. On the Day of Gathering (Mahshar), they will blame one another. Those who were arrogant will deny having led the weak astray. Yet they used to plot day and night. When they see the punishment, they will regret it, but it will be of no avail.\n\nNeither wealth nor children will bring you closer to Allah; only faith and righteous deeds bring one closer. The believers are in safety in Paradise. Those who strive against Our verses to invalidate them will be subjected to punishment. Allah extends provision for whom He wills and restricts it.\n\nOur Prophet was told: 'Stand up for Allah in pairs, in threes, and individually, and reflect.' There is no madness in him. He is only a warner. The truth has come, and falsehood has vanished. Those who do not believe will regret, but it will be too late. A barrier will be placed between them and what they desire, just as was done with their predecessors."